===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|nautical}} {{short for|en|Flemish coil|t=a [[rope#Noun|rope]] that has been [[arrange]]d into [[neat#Adjective|neat]], [[flat#Adjective|flat]] [[concentric]] [[coil#Noun|coils]]}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[George Cupples]|authorlink=George Cupples|chapter=An Event for Mr. Harry Spencer|title=Captain Herbert: A Sea Story.{{nb...|In Three Volumes.}}|location=London|publisher=[[w:Chapman & Hall|Chapman and Hall]],{{nb...|193, Piccadilly.}}|year=1864|volume=II|page=38|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=OqZDAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA38|oclc=681199109|passage=He really knew more of the ropes than half the mess did, though he had made no show; he could have managed to stow the mizen-royal in a squall by himself, if such had been now proper in his position; there were even two or three out-of-the-way knots, splices, and '''flemishes'''—practised by veteran A.B.'s alone, revealed to few, and only to be acquired with perfection in the studious solitude of the merchant service, with which Dick Diamond could be seen in sequestered corners to perplex raw hands of the waist or after-guard, he having singular instincts for that art—but some of which Harry could have explained in passing by.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|chapter=Deck Seamanship|title=The Bluejackets’ Manual: {{w|United States Navy}}|location=Annapolis, Md.|publisher={{w|United States Naval Institute}}|edition=10th|year=1940|pages=438–439|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/bluejacketsmanua00mcle/page/439/mode/1up|oclc=12776987|passage=Frequently fancy '''Flemishes''' are made, for example, with a regular Flemish coil in the center and the rest of the line placed in various shapes about it but always so as to retain the appearance of a mat.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Edmund A. Gibson|title=Basic Seamanship and Navigation|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:S&P Global|McGraw-Hill Book Co.]]|year=1951|page=363|oclc=250587978|passage=A coiled, faked, or flemished down line is said to be laid up in coils, fakes, or '''flemishes'''.}}

