===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive|nautical}} ''Often followed by'' '''[[down#Adverb|down]]''': to [[arrange]] (a [[rope#Noun|rope]]) into [[neat#Adjective|neat]], [[flat#Adjective|flat]] [[concentric]] [[coil#Noun|coils]].
#* {{RQ:Marryat Newton Forster|volume=I|chapter=XI|page=138|passage=In about twenty minutes, after the messenger had been stowed away, the cables coiled in the tiers, and the ropes '''flemished''' down on deck, the captain made his appearance, and directed the first-lieutenant to send aft the newly impressed men.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=More on Marlinespike|editor=[[w:Charles Frederic Chapman|Charles F[rederic] Chapman]]|magazine=Motor Boating: The Yachtsman’s Magazine|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Hearst Communications|Hearst Magazines]]|month=November|year=1943|volume=72|issue=5|page=32|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/sim_motor-boating_1943-11_72_5/page/32/mode/1up|column=2|oclc=688637276|passage=When great neatness is desired a line is '''flemished''' down. Successive circles of the line are wrapped about each other with the free end at the center. When it is finished it looks like a mat and with an old piece of line can be used as one. {{...}} If a line is '''flemished''' down and left on a deck for some time it will mark the deck as well as remain wet on the under side, and therefore deteriorate. On small boats lines are usually either coiled down or '''flemished''' down.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Catherine [Irvine] Gavin|chapter=The ''Molly-O''|title=The Fortress|location=Garden City, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Doubleday (publisher)|Doubleday & Company]]|year=1964|page=26|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/fortress00gavi/page/26/mode/1up|oclc=1353913|passage=The two Swedish sailors on deck, lounging and spitting into the water, seemed not to have been kept up to their duties, for paint work and bright work were far from brilliant, and the ropes were badly pointed and '''flemished'''.}}

